60,3,1,talk,Asuka,,"Option A is too risky socially. Option B is physically impractical. Option C, the lottery, seems the most logical for fairness.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Ryuji,,"I initially liked Option A for the funds, but @Shunichi's point about the neighbouring block is a strong warning.","{""words"":19,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,George,,I support Option C. It solves the historical bias I found in the minutes and treats everyone equally.,"{""words"":18,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Shunichi,,Option C is the safest. It's a fair gamble that doesn't turn neighbors into creditors and debtors.,"{""words"":17,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Mio,,Option B sounds like a maintenance nightmare with those racks. Option C is easy to run once a year.,"{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Ryuji, do you agree that the social cost of Option A outweighs the financial gain?","{""words"":15,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Ryuji,,Yes. A building where people stop attending meetings is a failed community. I'll vote for the lottery.,"{""words"":17,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,George,,It's settled then. The lottery is the most equitable path.,"{""words"":10,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Shunichi,,I agree. It's the only one that doesn't invite long-term resentment.,"{""words"":11,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Mio,,I'm in! Let's go with the lottery.,"{""words"":7,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
